1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,000
A relation is a set of tuples over one fixed schema.

2
00:00:10,000 --> 00:00:20,000
The tuple (2, y) arrives twice, and the set keeps it once.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:30,000
Six operators generate everything else in this lesson.

4
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,000
Selection keeps the rows that pass a test; the schema is unchanged.

5
00:00:40,000 --> 00:00:50,000
Projection keeps the columns you name, and drops repeated rows.

6
00:00:50,000 --> 00:01:00,000
SQL keeps duplicates, so the algebra and SQL differ by DISTINCT.

7
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:10,000
Union needs the same schema on both sides, in the same order.

8
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:20,000
Difference keeps the rows of the left operand that are not on the right.

9
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:30,000
The product pairs every row with every row: 3 times 2 gives 6 rows.

10
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:40,000
A theta join is a product followed by a selection on the pair.

11
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:50,000
A theta join keeps both copies of the joined attribute; a natural join merges them.

12
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:00,000
Outer joins keep unmatched rows and fill the other side with NULL.

13
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:10,000
The question is: which students took every course that SA took?

14
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:20,000
Four students survive: SA, SC, SD and SF, so T2 has 4 rows.

15
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Division needs no new operator: projection, product and difference suffice.

16
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:40,000
Options A and B are the two standard spellings of division.

17
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:50,000
Option C divides by the brands that happen to be owned, and returns E1.

18
00:02:50,000 --> 00:03:00,000
Option D invents every pair first, and returns E1 and E2 on the second witness.

19
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:10,000
A calculus expression is a set former with a logical condition.

20
00:03:10,000 --> 00:03:20,000
A variable shared by two relations becomes an equality condition.

21
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:30,000
GATE 2026 Set 1 Q43: the answer is option B, P of S joined on X equal to Q.

22
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:40,000
FROM builds the product, WHERE filters rows, SELECT chooses columns.

23
00:03:40,000 --> 00:03:50,000
GROUP BY forms groups; HAVING filters groups, never individual rows.

24
00:03:50,000 --> 00:04:00,000
pi is SELECT DISTINCT, sigma is WHERE, the product is a comma in FROM.

25
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:10,000
A comparison with NULL is UNKNOWN, and WHERE keeps only TRUE.

26
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:20,000
On the values 6, 5 and NULL, x = 5 and x <> 5 return one row each.

27
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:30,000
NOT IN over a subquery holding a NULL returns no rows at all.

28
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:40,000
A one-to-many relationship puts its foreign key on the many side.

29
00:04:40,000 --> 00:04:50,000
Six relations come out of this design, and every key is accounted for.

30
00:04:50,000 --> 00:05:00,000
Write the result schema first, then the tuples, and check NULLs last.

